Z||000000||ka4nkf||10-21-2000||01:11 PM||||There Is a tab above the window toward the front that protudes outward about an inch. It Is attached to the top of the car, and does not touch the window at all. I have noticed several different cars that have this, but I cant find anyone to tell me what it Is for. I always thought it was to keep the window tight but It does not touch the window.||||reg||1
Z||000001||contra||10-21-2000||01:50 PM||||that would be the tab that keeps the window from closing improperly. The fiero windows roll up straight and that little tab makes sure they curve correctly so that the window seals tight. otherwise it would just go straight up and you'd have a crack in the window seal :P<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-21-2000||05:21 PM||||Pretty much what contra said. It's just to make sure the top of the window lines up in the seal. If the window is adjusted right then it usually dosn't hit it much if at all. The only time you're likely to hit one of those is just as the window is closing when driving on rough roads. It protects the seal more than the window. ((The metal version of these can actually damage the window when the coating comes off.))<P>While it looks like the window goes straight up that's not quite what it does. As it goes up the top moves inward. This causes considerable load on the guide pads below the outer dew strip. That loading is a big chunk of why the outer pads do so much damage when they wear out or get dirty.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||10-21-2000||05:33 PM||||Thanks orge and contra. you may be right but my windows close perfect, and seal perfect, no water leakage when washing the car. but when my windows are rolled up they dont even come within a quarter of an inch hitting that tab. so I am still confused.||||reg||1
